WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:08.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:09.960
Hey, everyone.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.250
My name Sandeep, and I'm a
developer advocate at Google.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.670
Today I'll be bringing
you some quick tips

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:18.320
to get started on
Google Compute Engine.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.330
Setting up your Google
Cloud Platform account.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.110
Before we jump
into doing things,

00:00:23.110 --> 00:00:24.860
let's set up your account.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:28.460
If you already have one,
you can just skip forward.

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:31.120
The first thing you need
is a Google account.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.940
If you use Gmail, Google+,
YouTube, or Android,

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.500
you already have one of these.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.270
When you sign up
for the trial, it'll

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.470
ask you for credit
card information.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.750
But we won't charge you unless
you upgrade your account.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.460
And don't worry about your code.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.590
We automatically
pause your account

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:49.410
after the free
trial is over so you

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.960
can upgrade when you're ready.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.090
You can see a free trial
page for more information.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.340
Using the Cloud Launcher to
quickly deploy WordPress.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:01.330
So you want to get your
website up and running fast.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.610
The best way to do this is
to use the Cloud Launcher.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.120
Here you can select
preconfigured images

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.930
and launch them in
just a few clicks.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:12.990
Let's go to cloud.google.com
to get started.

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.680
Make sure you are logged
into your Google account.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:18.650
If you can see your picture
and the My Console link,

00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:20.980
you're good to go.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.990
Now click the Launcher link.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:25.660
Welcome to the Cloud Launcher.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.370
The launcher has hundreds
of preconfigured images

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:30.740
from Google and
partners such as Bitnami

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.080
that you can launch
with just a few clicks.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.970
In our case, we want to
start a WordPress website.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.140
So we click on the
WordPress Google Click

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.710
to Deploy solution.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.490
Here we can see typical
cost for the solution

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.220
and what is included.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.720
By default, it launches
an n1-standard-1 machine

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.940
with an additional
100 gigabytes of disk

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:56.680
and the estimated monthly
cost is around $35.84.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.000
Using the Pricing Calculator
to estimate pricing.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.140
Before we keep going,
I'm going to show you

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.850
how you can customize your
estimate by using the pricing

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:06.090
calculator.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.150
While the free trial makes
it easy to experiment

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.270
until you find your
perfect solution,

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:13.540
it's a good idea to use
the pricing calculator

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:15.470
to estimate your costs.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:19.160
This way you can create a
combination that works for you.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:22.990
On cloud.google.com,
click the Pricing link.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:27.490
Then click on Get
a Custom Quote.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:30.530
Here you can play with
different configurations

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.870
to get the set up that
fits your budget and needs.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:37.560
For this example, I'm going
to be hosting a small website.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:41.010
So I have one server.

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:45.210
And I select a free operating
system and a g1 small instance

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:47.820
type.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.300
The server is going to be
running all day and all night,

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:51.600
so these numbers look good.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.490
So I also like the
g1 small instance

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.040
with 50 gigabytes of disk space.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.550
The monthly cost comes
to around $18.35.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:05.510
This looks great, but remember
it is only an estimate,

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.200
and your actual cost can vary.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.080
Now that we found
the image we want

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.950
to deploy and the estimated
costs, it's time to deploy it.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.750
Let's go back to
the Cloud Launcher

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.110
and select the WordPress image.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.480
The first thing we want to
do is click Get Started.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.820
Now, select the project
you want to deploy into

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.180
or create a new one.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.300
I've already created
one for this example,

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:33.060
so I'm going to select that one.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.440
Now we see the
configuration page.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:37.610
For the Google Click
to Deploy solutions,

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.990
the configuration section
will be customized

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:41.720
for that particular solution.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.270
For WordPress, the options
are rather straightforward.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.400
I can change my
zone if I want, then

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.520
I select the g1
small instance like I

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.590
configured in the
pricing section

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:58.560
and set the disk
to 50 gigabytes.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.700
Then I type in my admin email.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:06.190
I also make sure that
phpmyadmin is checked.

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:10.770
Once I click Deploy WordPress,
the wheels start turning.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.480
As you can see, deployment
can take around four to five

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.740
minutes to complete,
so I'm going

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.800
to fast forward this section.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:20.860
After a few minutes, we can
see our WordPress instance

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.650
is up and running.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.550
Configuring firewalls
and networks.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.270
Now that the
instance is running,

00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:29.830
I'm going to show
you how to access it.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:32.130
The first step is to
configure the firewall

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:34.430
to allow HTTP traffic.

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:41.230
Go to cloud.google.com, then
select the My Console link.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.830
Select the project
you are working on.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:49.675
Click Compute, Compute
Engine, and VM Instances.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:58.635
Click the External IP and
check Enable HTTP option.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.650
If you want to make
some more complex rules,

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.275
we've got to visit
the Network section.

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.760
Click Network on
the side panel, then

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.830
select the network
you want to modify.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:18.800
In most cases, you just need
to modify the default network.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:24.120
Here, we can see all the rules
for the whole default network.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.980
You may notice that HTTP
rule we made is not here,

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:32.210
and that is because that rule is
only for the specific machine.

00:05:32.210 --> 00:05:34.560
If you want all machines
on the default network

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.260
to expose port 80, this is
the best place to do it.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:45.620
So let's add a firewall rule
and then call the rule HTTP.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:52.900
Then type 0.0.0.0/0 to allow
anyone to access our website

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:58.640
and open up TCP port 80, which
is the standard HTTP port.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:00.500
Click Create.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.350
Now, all machines on the default
network have port 80 open.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.320
To delete a Click To
Deploy deployment,

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.050
go to your Developer Console.

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:20.540
Go to the Manage section,
and then go to Deployments.

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:22.980
Click the Trash icon to
delete the deployment.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.150
While Click to Deploy
is awesome and includes

00:06:32.150 --> 00:06:36.520
a lot of prebuilt stacks like
MEAN, LAMP, LAMP Rails, and all

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.150
the Bitnami images,
sometimes you

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.990
want to roll your
own custom set up.

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:46.090
Google Cloud Platform
makes this easy to do.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.500
Before we go any further,
I want to mention

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:50.800
that Google Cloud has
an awesome command line

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.320
interface and the REST
interface that you

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.830
can use to create, deploy, and
manage pretty much anything

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:01.360
on the platform from VMs
to storage to databases.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:04.470
You can see how this can
make automation really easy.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.810
For this video, I'll be doing
mostly everything from the GUI,

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:10.720
but you might notice little
equivalent Rest and Command

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.110
Line links down below.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:16.170
So everything I'm doing
can be replicated via REST

00:07:16.170 --> 00:07:18.610
on Command Line as well.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:21.490
The first thing I'm going
to do is go to My Console.

00:07:21.490 --> 00:07:25.790
Then I'll go to the compute
section and the VM instances

00:07:25.790 --> 00:07:26.990
section.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.030
Now click Create Instance.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:32.906
Here we can see the
config page, and we

00:07:32.906 --> 00:07:35.550
can give our instance a
name and choose a zone.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:37.620
It's a good idea to
choose a zone that's

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.570
close to your customers.

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:42.400
Then we can pick a machine type.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.230
Pick the right machine
type for your workload.

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:49.200
I'm going to pick an
n1-standard1 to start with.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.870
Now we choose the
boot image and disk.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:54.050
You can see all the
options we offer,

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:57.900
from Debian to Windows Server,
to Enterprise Red Hat Linux.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:07:59.730
You can even create
your own image

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:02.370
or use a previous snapshot.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:07.540
I like Ubuntu 14.04 LTS so
that's what I'm going to use.

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:10.520
I also want it to use
an SSD disk, which

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:14.010
is much faster, although
slightly more expensive.

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:18.320
We can also open the HTTP
and HTTPS ports right here,

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:21.800
saving us a trip to
the Network section.

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:24.680
At this point, we
can create our VM.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:29.180
But I want to talk about the
advanced optional settings.

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:31.080
If we had a fleet
of machines, we

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:34.880
can give each one individual
tags to categorize them.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.605
You can also specify a startup
script that will run on boot.

00:08:38.605 --> 00:08:40.230
I like to set up a
script that installs

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:42.049
Docker and automatically
pulls and starts

00:08:42.049 --> 00:08:46.140
my containers without
having me type anything.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:48.610
In the Disk section, you
can add additional disks

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:49.990
to your machine.

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:51.690
I highly recommend
creating a disk

00:08:51.690 --> 00:08:54.900
for storing application data
and not storing your application

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.560
data on the boot disk.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.370
In the Network
section, you can choose

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:02.390
if you want temporary IP
address or a static IP address.

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:04.060
The temporary
address will change

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:06.100
every time you
reboot your machine,

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.020
while the static
address stays the same,

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.978
and you can actually move
it between instances.

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:15.720
I like the static
addresses because it

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.790
makes configuring
DNS and upgrading

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.450
machine types that much easier.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:24.840
Note, you will be charged
for unused static addresses,

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:28.450
so make sure you use
them if you make them.

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:32.440
In the Axis and Security
section you can add SSH keys

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:35.930
and enable access to other
Google Cloud Platform APIs

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:39.650
by default.

00:09:39.650 --> 00:09:42.850
Now let's hit Create, and
you can see it chugging away

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:44.430
in the background.

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.330
In a few seconds,
and I'm serious,

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:48.090
the VM is up and running.

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:52.340
We get an SSH into our box by
clicking the little SSH button.

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:55.650
If we launch the Windows Server,
we would get an RDP option

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:57.170
instead.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:59.950
You can also download
an SSH key to use

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:02.100
or if you've installed
the Google Cloud

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:05.390
SDK that I previously mentioned,
you can get SSH directly

00:10:05.390 --> 00:10:06.440
from there as well.

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:13.570
Now you can do anything
you want with your VM.

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:15.870
Backups, increasing
disk capacity,

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:18.220
and upgrading servers.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.530
Now that your server is up and
running and getting popular,

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:25.810
it's important to know how to
back up your VM and upgrade it.

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:28.980
Thankfully, Google Compute
Engine makes it easy.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:31.450
To make a backup of
your disk, all you do

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:36.820
is go to the snapshot section,
click Create Snapshot,

00:10:36.820 --> 00:10:38.470
select the disk, and bam.

00:10:38.470 --> 00:10:42.590
After a few seconds,
a snapshot is created.

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:45.620
And using the REST or
Command Line interface,

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.650
you can easily
automate this process.

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:50.630
You can use this snapshot to
create new virtual machines

00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:54.860
or use it to restore your
server to a previous state.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:58.070
Now let's look at how we
can upgrade a machine. .

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:00.020
I want to do four things.

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:04.440
1) Attach a new 100 gigabyte
disk for application data.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:07.750
2) Grow this new disk
to 500 gigabytes.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:13.560
3) Grow my boot disk from 10
gigabytes of 50 gigabytes.

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:18.030
4) Go from a 1 core machine
to a 4 core machine.

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:22.000
1) Attach a new disk
to a running instance.

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:24.500
Let's look at how we can
attach a new blank disk

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:25.750
to a running instance.

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:28.480
Click the instance you
want to attach the disk to,

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.975
then go to the disk section
and select New Disk.

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:39.640
Let's set it to
use an empty image

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.190
with 100 gigabytes of space.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.710
Now that the disk is
physically attached to the VM,

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.830
we need to mount it.

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.960
For the rest of
the tutorial, I'm

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:58.040
going to be running Linux
commands on an Ubuntu system.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:01.500
These commands should work
for most Linux systems,

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:04.770
but your particular machine
might be a little different.

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:07.990
SSH into the machine by
clicking the SSH button.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:17.350
Then let's look at all the disks
using the LS block command.

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:20.620
Here we can see
our boot disk SDA 1

00:12:20.620 --> 00:12:22.760
is mounted as the
root directory.

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:26.090
We can also see a
new disk called SDB,

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:27.540
and there's no mount point.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:28.450
Let's fix that.

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:31.800
First, make a new directory
to mount our disk.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:34.310
Let's call it mount app data.

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:38.620
Run sudo mkdir mnt app data
to make our new directory.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.710
Then Google Cloud
Platform provides a script

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:48.120
to automatically format
the disk and mount it.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:52.370
Run this command, which will
format and mount the disk.

00:12:52.370 --> 00:12:54.460
If the disk was
preformatted, we could

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:56.110
use the standard
mount command here.

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:14.880
Once that is done,
we can run lsblk

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:16.240
and see the disk is mounted.

00:13:19.960 --> 00:13:24.400
2) Increase the size of
an attached data disk.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:26.910
Let's say you want to
increase the application disk

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:30.140
size from 100 gigabytes
to 500 gigabytes

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:32.720
because your data is growing.

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:35.950
The first thing you want
to do is stop your server.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:38.140
This will try to
perform a clean shutdown

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:41.500
so you don't lose any data that
might be written to the disk.

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:43.650
then create a
snapshot of the disk.

00:13:49.070 --> 00:13:51.860
After this is done,
go to the disk section

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:53.210
and create a new disk.

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:59.150
It is very important that
you create this new disk

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:02.030
in the same zone as the VM.

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:04.940
Select the snapshot we just
created and set the size

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:05.905
to 500 gigabytes.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:19.790
Now let's go to the Instances
screen, click our server,

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:22.540
remove the old disk
from the instance,

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:23.870
and attach the new one.

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:38.700
Now, if you boot
up your machine,

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:42.940
we can run lsblk command,
and the disk is there.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:45.590
We can mount it using the
following mount command.

00:14:54.230 --> 00:14:57.270
And if you run
the df -h command,

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:59.670
you can see the disk
space has not increased.

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:01.680
It still says 100 gigabytes.

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:06.060
This is because the snapshot we
used is set to old disk size.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:10.620
We need to resize the partition
to use the full 500 gigabytes.

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:15.770
First, unmount the drive with
sudo umount mnt app data.

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:18.430
While you technically
can resize a drive that's

00:15:18.430 --> 00:15:22.150
currently mounted, it is
safer to unmount the drive.

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:28.580
Run sudo e2fsck -f and the
name the drive, sdb, then

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:33.240
run sudo resize2fs and the
name of the drive, again, sdb.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:35.300
After that is done,
run the mount command

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:36.910
again to remount your drive.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:47.940
Now you can see we get the
full 500 gigabytes of disk,

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:49.600
and it only took four commands.

00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:51.950
Easy.

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:55.870
Re-size the boot disk and
upgrade your machine type.

00:15:55.870 --> 00:15:59.040
In order to upgrade your
boot disk or upgrade your VM,

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:02.150
you need to follow
basically the same process.

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:04.950
This process requires you to
create a brand-new instance

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:07.330
and delete the old one.

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:11.890
First, make sure
your VM is stopped.

00:16:11.890 --> 00:16:14.620
Then create a snapshot
of the boot disk.

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:22.460
Then create a new disk
using the snapshot.

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:27.240
Make sure the zone
and size are correct.

00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:40.460
Now we need to delete
the old instance.

00:16:40.460 --> 00:16:44.340
Double check that the
snapshot and disk are created,

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:46.910
because deletion is permanent.

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:49.550
After this is done, we need
to create a new instance

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:50.710
to use this new disk.

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:56.320
Let's select a four
core machine and use

00:16:56.320 --> 00:16:57.580
the new disk we just created.

00:17:04.839 --> 00:17:07.349
Remember to attach the
old data disk as well.

00:17:07.349 --> 00:17:11.030
Once the machine is created, we
need to reuse our old static IP

00:17:11.030 --> 00:17:15.780
address so we know how to
reconfigure any DNS settings.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:20.010
Go to the Network section and
select the static IP address.

